  "before_interfaces": {
    "lem-tgr-positive-damping-boundary": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume Countable Choice and use Euclidean Lebesgue measure. Let $m\\ge0$. For $m=0$ take $\\mathbb R^0$ to be a singleton with measure $1$. For each sufficiently small $\\epsilon>0$ let $d_\\epsilon:\\mathbb R^m\\times\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ be smooth and real-valued. Assume that for every compact $V\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ and every $R>0$ there are constants $c,C,M,\\epsilon_0>0$ such that, on $V\\times[-R,R]$ and for $0<\\epsilon<\\epsilon_0$,\n$$c\\le d_\\epsilon(v,s)\\le C,\\qquad |\\partial_s d_\\epsilon(v,s)|+|\\partial_s^2d_\\epsilon(v,s)|\\le M.$$\nFor $\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb C)$ set $\\widehat\\varphi(k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\varphi(s)e^{-iks}\\,ds$ and define $B$ by\n$$\\langle B,\\varphi\\rangle:=\\int_0^\\infty k\\widehat\\varphi(k)\\,dk.$$\nFor $\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^m\\times\\mathbb R;\\mathbb C)$ write $\\widehat\\psi(v,k)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\psi(v,s)e^{-iks}\\,ds$ and define $K$ by\n$$\\langle K,\\psi\\rangle:=\\int_{\\mathbb R^m}\\int_0^\\infty k\\widehat\\psi(v,k)\\,dk\\,dv.$$\nThen $B$ and $K$ are distributions, $B$ is the lower boundary denoted $-(s-i0)^{-2}$, and $K=1\\otimes B$ in the explicitly defined pairing above. The smooth kernels\n$$K_\\epsilon(v,s):=-\\bigl(s-i\\epsilon d_\\epsilon(v,s)\\bigr)^{-2}$$\nconverge to $K$ in the distributional sense: $\\int K_\\epsilon(v,s)\\psi(v,s)\\,dv\\,ds\\to\\langle K,\\psi\\rangle$ for every such $\\psi$. When $s$ and $\\epsilon$ carry units of length, $d_\\epsilon$ is dimensionless."
    ],
    "lem-tgr-exponential-current": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume Countable Choice. Let\n$$\\mathcal H=L^2((0,\\infty),dk;\\mathbb C),\\qquad \\langle h,q\\rangle_2=\\int_0^\\infty\\overline{h(k)}q(k)\\,dk,$$\nusing the second-variable-linear convention. For\n$f\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb C)=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb C)$ define\n$$h_f(k)=\\sqrt{\\frac{k}{4\\pi}}\\int_{\\mathbb R}f(U)e^{ikU}\\,dU\\quad(k>0).$$\nThen $h_f,h_{\\bar f}\\in\\mathcal H$, and $f\\mapsto h_f$ is continuous on each fixed-compact-support test space. With the bosonic Fock conventions of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]], define on its finite-particle domain\n$$J(f)=a(h_{\\bar f})+a^\\dagger(h_f).$$\nThis is a complex-linear operator-valued distribution on that invariant domain. For real $f$, its closure is $\\overline{J(f)}=\\sqrt2\\,\\Phi(h_f)$, where $\\Phi(h_f)$ is the closed Segal field of [[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]]; the corresponding Weyl one-parameter group has generator $i\\Phi(h_f)$. The vacuum two-point form is positive type and has boundary kernel\n$$C(s)=-\\frac{1}{4\\pi(s-i0)^2},$$\nwhere $s-i0$ means the distributional limit from $s-i\\epsilon$, $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$.\n\nFor $\\kappa>0$ with units of inverse length, let $u,U$ have units of length and set\n$$U_\\kappa(u)=-\\kappa^{-1}e^{-\\kappa u},\\qquad u_\\kappa(U)=-\\kappa^{-1}\\log(-\\kappa U)\\quad(U<0).$$\nFor $g\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R;\\mathbb C)$ put $f_g(U)=g(u_\\kappa(U))$ for $U<0$ and $f_g(U)=0$ for $U\\ge0$, and define $j_\\kappa(g)=J(f_g)$. The zero extension is smooth and compactly supported. Its vacuum covariance is the positive-type distribution\n$$C_\\kappa(s)=-\\frac{\\kappa^2}{16\\pi}\\,\\operatorname{csch}^2\\!\\left(\\frac{\\kappa}{2}(s-i0)\\right).$$\nHere the notation is fixed as follows: for $b=\\kappa/2$, set $Y_b(s)=\\sinh(bs)/b$. Since $Y_b'(s)=\\cosh(bs)>0$ and $Y_b(s)\\to\\pm\\infty$ as $s\\to\\pm\\infty$, it is a smooth increasing diffeomorphism. Define $\\operatorname{csch}^2(b(s-i0)):=b^{-2}Y_b^*((y-i0)^{-2})$, where\n$$\\langle Y_b^*T,\\varphi\\rangle:=\\left\\langle T,\\varphi(Y_b^{-1}(y))(Y_b^{-1})'(y)\\right\\rangle.$$\nThis is a one-dimensional chiral test-coordinate model. It asserts no restriction of a four-dimensional field to a null curve."
    ],
    "lem-tgr-thermal-contour-spectrum": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume Countable Choice. Let $A,b>0$, put $\\beta=\\pi/b$ and $L=A/b^2$, and define the analytic kernel\n$$C(z)=-A\\operatorname{csch}^2(bz),\\qquad \\operatorname{csch}w=1/\\sinh w.$$\nUse the angular-frequency convention\n$$\\widehat\\varphi(\\omega)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\varphi(s)e^{-i\\omega s}\\,ds,\\qquad \\varphi(s)=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\widehat\\varphi(\\omega)e^{i\\omega s}\\,d\\omega$$\nfor Schwartz functions $\\varphi\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R)$, where Schwartz space has seminorms $\\sup_s|s^m\\varphi^{(n)}(s)|$ and a tempered distribution is a continuous complex-linear functional on that space. Define its Fourier transform by the bilinear pairing $\\langle\\widehat T,\\varphi\\rangle=\\langle T,\\widehat\\varphi\\rangle$; test functions are not conjugated.\n\nFor $0<\\epsilon<\\beta$, $C_\\epsilon(s)=C(s-i\\epsilon)$ is a smooth $L^1$ function. These regularizations have a limit $C_{A,b}\\in\\mathcal S'(\\mathbb R)$ as $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$, denoted $-A\\operatorname{csch}^2(b(s-i0))$. The kernel is holomorphic on $-\\beta<\\operatorname{Im}z<0$, has period $i\\beta$, and its tested boundary values obey\n$$C(s-i\\beta+i0)=C(-s-i0).$$\nThe Fourier transform of $C_{A,b}$ is the continuous nonnegative function\n$$S_{A,b}(\\omega)=\\frac{2\\pi(A/b^2)\\omega}{e^{\\pi\\omega/b}-1},\\qquad S_{A,b}(0)=\\frac{2\\pi L}{\\beta}=\\frac{2A}{b}.$$\nIn particular $S_{A,b}(-\\omega)=e^{\\beta\\omega}S_{A,b}(\\omega)$ for $\\omega>0$. The zero-frequency value is the removable limit, and the boundary and Fourier assertions are distributional, not pointwise integrals at a double pole.\n\nIf the coordinate has a specified unit, $s,z,\\beta,\\epsilon$ have that unit and $b,\\omega$ its reciprocal. If $A$ has covariance units, $L$ has those units times the square of the coordinate unit and $S_{A,b}$ has those units times the coordinate unit.\n\nIn particular, for $\\kappa>0$ in inverse length, take $A=\\kappa^2/(16\\pi)$ and $b=\\kappa/2$. Then $L=1/(4\\pi)$, $\\beta=2\\pi/\\kappa$, and $C_{A,b}$ is precisely the tested chiral covariance $C_\\kappa$ of [[lem-tgr-exponential-current]]. Its spectrum and detailed balance are\n$$S_\\kappa(\\omega)=\\frac{\\omega}{2(e^{2\\pi\\omega/\\kappa}-1)},\\qquad S_\\kappa(0)=\\frac{\\kappa}{4\\pi},\\qquad S_\\kappa(-\\omega)=e^{2\\pi\\omega/\\kappa}S_\\kappa(\\omega)\\quad(\\omega>0).$$\nHere $C_\\kappa$ has units of inverse length squared and $S_\\kappa$ inverse length. This is a two-point strip and detailed-balance statement, including the specified chiral current as a specialization. It does not assert a norm-continuous $C^*$-algebra KMS dynamics or an exact detector transition probability."
    ],
    "ex-tgr-chiral-generator-rescaling": [
